2. CLINICAL AND SCIENTIFIC EVIDENCE\
--------------------------------------------------\
\
Create a research-supported evidence section.\
\
Review the following examples:\
\
A. ANAMORELIN / ROMANA 1 AND ROMANA 2\
\
Explain how improvements in lean body mass did not\
translate into demonstrated improvements in handgrip\
strength.\
\
Review the EMA's reasons for refusing authorization,\
including both the clinical-benefit and safety-data concerns.\
\
Explain why endpoint selection and evidence of meaningful\
clinical benefit matter in drug development.\
\
Do not suggest a different measurement would necessarily\
have changed the regulatory outcome.\
\
B. PONSEGROMAB / CANCER CACHEXIA\
\
Review the published Phase II study.\
\
Describe:\
\
- Clinical indication\
- Study population\
- Primary endpoint\
- Relevant secondary endpoints\
- Role of GDF-15\
- Digital physical activity measurements\
\
Explain how biological, symptomatic, and functional\
measurements can provide complementary evidence.\
\
C. CAMERA-BASED PHYSICAL ASSESSMENT VALIDATION\
\
Review:\
\
Bertrand et al., PLOS Digital Health, January 2026.\
\
This study evaluated automated physical-performance\
assessments in 228 adults.\
\
Extract:\
\
- Population\
- Technology used\
- Assessments performed\
- Agreement with clinical reference measurements\
- Data loss\
- Limitations\
- Remaining validation needs\
\
Explicitly acknowledge that automated camera-based\
functional assessment already exists.\
\
Identify what MotionVitals would need to do differently\
to create a viable product.\
\
D. DIGITAL ENDPOINT REGULATORY PRECEDENTS\
\
Review:\
\
- FDA digitally derived measures framework\
- FDA clinical outcome assessment guidance\
- FDA digital health technology clinical investigation guidance\
- EMA qualification of wearable-derived stride velocity\
